{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504880047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEyNDA4NjkxMjM="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1504880047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEyNDA4NjkxMjU="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1515711729,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE0MjA3ODc3NTM="},"added":["good-for-hackathon"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1523043788,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3OTM1NzE2OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1810#issuecomment-379357168"},"message":"`% datalad install ///labs/haxby/raiders@golden_release5//sub*/anat`","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1523045922,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM3OTM2NTc3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1810#issuecomment-379365779"},"message":"on one hand I like the conciseness of the `@` semantic used by some projects within urls to state the \"version\".\nBut\n- tag could contain / (e.g. typical in debian packages to have `debian/` prefix for debian release tags), which, if `@tag` used in the middle of the url, makes it \"obscure\" to say the least (we could rely on it to be followed by `//` - tag could not contain it)\n- `@` is not a legit character within url, so not sure yet if we would not need some special handling for the URLs containing it, let's see:\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import URL; print(URL(\"http://datasets.datalad.org/labs/haxby/raiders@golden_release5//sub*/anat\").fields)'\nOrderedDict([('path', '/labs/haxby/raiders@golden_release5//sub*/anat'), ('scheme', 'http'), ('username', ''), ('password', ''), ('hostname', 'datasets.datalad.org'), ('port', ''), ('query', ''), ('fragment', '')])\n```\nso seems to be \"ok'ish\", but we seems to fail to identify correctly (thinks it is SSHRI... yikes) which would then need to be fixed up (seems needs more than just disallowing `/` in username.. could look later)\n```shell\n$\u003e python -c 'from datalad.support.network import RI; print(RI(\"///labs/haxby/raiders@golden_release5//sub*/anat\").fields)'                                         \nOrderedDict([('path', ''), ('username', '///labs/haxby/raiders'), ('hostname', 'golden_release5//sub*/anat'), ('port', '')])\n```\n- not clear how to say that it should be a specific branch instead of a fixed committish (other use-cases of @ in url to identify commit didn't really care to point to a branch, then needed a static version definition).\n\nas an alternative it could be an uglier but more descriptive\n```///labs/haxby/raiders//sub*/anat?version=golden_release5``` while allowing for specifying a branch\n```///future/hcp//sub*/anat?branch=hcp900``` possibly also with specific version ```///future/hcp//sub*/anat?version=hcp900.1\u0026branch=hcp900```\n\nin case we decide to fix RIs handling, here is a quick one:\n```patch\ndiff --git a/datalad/support/network.py b/datalad/support/network.py\nindex caa517e8..7502e5ca 100644\n--- a/datalad/support/network.py\n+++ b/datalad/support/network.py\n@@ -315,7 +315,9 @@ def _guess_ri_cls(ri):\n \n     if not fields['scheme'] and not fields['hostname']:\n         parts = _split_colon(ri)\n-        if fields['path'] and '@' in fields['path'] or len(parts) \u003e 1:\n+        if fields['path'] \\\n+                and ('@' in fields['path'] and not ri.startswith('//')) \\\n+                or len(parts) \u003e 1:\n             # user@host:path/sp1\n             # or host_name: (hence parts check)\n             # TODO: we need a regex to catch those really, parts check is not suff\n@@ -714,7 +716,7 @@ class SSHRI(RI, RegexBasedURLMixin):\n         'port',\n     )\n \n-    _REGEX = re.compile(r'((?P\u003cusername\u003e\\S*)@)?(?P\u003chostname\u003e[^:]+)(\\:(?P\u003cpath\u003e.*))?$')\n+    _REGEX = re.compile(r'((?P\u003cusername\u003e[^ \\t@/]*)@)?(?P\u003chostname\u003e[^/:]+)(\\:(?P\u003cpath\u003e.*))?$')\n \n     @classmethod\n     def _normalize_fields(cls, fields):\n```","files":null}]}